-- title:
--   Artin reciprocity at an admissible modulus of ℓ-power degree
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $L$ be number fields with $L/K$ Galois and with $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K) = (L \simeq_{\mathrm{alg}[K]} L)$ commutative, let $\ell$ be a prime and $k$ a natural number, and assume $x^{\ell^k} = 1$ for every $x \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$ (so $\ell^k$ annihilates the group, not necessarily being its exact exponent). Let $\mathfrak f$ be an ideal of $\mathcal O_K$ that is an admissible modulus of degree $\ell^k$ for $L/K$, that is: $\mathfrak f \neq 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_K$ whose chosen prime `primeAbove K L v` of $\mathcal O_L$ has nontrivial inertia subgroup in $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$, the power $v^{e}$ divides $\mathfrak f$, where $e = 1 + \sum_{p \mid \ell^k} (v_p(\ell^k) + 1)\, e(v \mid p)$ with $e(v\mid p)$ the ramification index of $v$ over $p\mathbb Z$ (so $e = 1 + (k+1)e(v\mid \ell)$ when $k \geq 1$, and $e = 1$ when $k = 0$). Then the Artin symbol `artinSymbol K L 𝔣`, the homomorphism from the group of units of the group of fractional ideals of $K$ with $v$-adic valuation $0$ at every $v$ dividing $\mathfrak f$ to $\operatorname{Gal}(L/K)$ determined by sending each prime $v$ to the arithmetic Frobenius at `primeAbove K L v`, is surjective, and its kernel equals the norm-ray subgroup `normRaySubgroup K L 𝔣`: the join, inside the group of ideals coprime to $\mathfrak f$, of the narrow ray subgroup `narrowRaySubgroup K 𝔣` (the subgroup generated by `narrowRaySet K 𝔣`) with the image of the relative norm map `relNormCTM K L 𝔣` from the fractional ideals of $L$ coprime to the extended modulus `modulusExt K L 𝔣`.
--
--   This is Artin's reciprocity law in the form 'the Artin map modulo $\mathfrak f$ is surjective with kernel the norm-ray subgroup', for abelian extensions of exponent dividing a prime power $\ell^k$ and moduli divisible to a prescribed depth at the ramified primes. It is the main global input used by [`LanglandsTunnell.Artin.artinSymbol_surjective_and_ker_eq_normRaySubgroup_of_pow_eq_one_of_isAdmissibleModulusOfDegree`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.Artin.artinSymbol_surjective_and_ker_eq_normRaySubgroup_of_pow_eq_one_of_isAdmissibleModulusOfDegree), and it rests on the two class-field-theoretic inequalities together with the local statement that units sufficiently close to $1$ are $\ell^k$-th powers.
